    "created_at": "2016-09-26T12:28:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-09-26T12:28:12Z",
    "body": "I'm happy with firing an event; it does seem nice to be consistent with origin check failure.\n\nI don't think the algorithm as-is would work if we move it to run-a-worker, as the step 6.7 in which this check occurs specifically is meant to cause \"run a worker\" in step 8 to never happen.\n\nIt is unclear to me if avoiding sync lookup of state that could theoretically be in another process can work here at all. Step 6.5 is basically asking for access to the global scope that, in @annevk's mental model I think, lives in another process. That global scope is then consulted and modified a lot in step 6.7's substeps.\n\nSo I don't think the IPC thing is a good argument for moving to an error event, at least not until we understand much better how Firefox implements this. (And I'd say Chrome too, but @mikewest says that Chrome's implementation is somewhat busted.) But consistency might be a good argument.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
